Vendored reference code for the inference half of the `fdid_hongkong` benchmark.

Why a second bundle
  The sibling bundle, fdid_hongkong/, runs the author's Fun_FDID.R. That file
  returns the fit, the R^2 path, the selected controls and the ATT, but it
  computes no inference at all -- no standard error, interval, p-value or
  standardised statistic. Her MATLAB driver does. This bundle runs the MATLAB
  side so the whole inference path is pinned against the author's own code
  instead of against a reading of Proposition 2.1.

Files
  FDID_Matlab.m   Kathleen T. Li's driver: reads GDP.csv, runs the forward
                  selection, and computes the ATT, standard error, 95%
                  interval, one- and two-sided p-values and the standardised
                  ATT for both Forward DiD and conventional DiD. Verbatim.
  FDID_newR2.m    Her forward-selection subroutine, called by the driver.
                  Verbatim.
  normcdf.m       A shim, not the author's. Base GNU Octave does not ship
                  NORMCDF (it lives in the statistics package, and in MATLAB in
                  the Statistics Toolbox). The driver calls it only for the
                  standard normal, and this is that CDF in closed form,
                  Phi(x) = erfc(-x/sqrt(2))/2 -- exact to machine precision,
                  so the captured values are the author's numbers. Vendored so
                  her driver runs unmodified.
  reference.m     The runner: puts the above on the path, changes into
                  fdid_hongkong/ (which holds GDP.csv), runs FDID_Matlab
                  unmodified, and prints the captured values.

Data
  GDP.csv is not duplicated here. The copy under fdid_hongkong/ is
  byte-identical to the replication package's (sha256
  487c7007cad0ee7b59e3fd315004edab64641f310ede7aeb003707f905615e55), so both
  bundles pin the same panel and provenance.json records the one checksum.

Headless plotting
  FDID_Matlab.m ends by drawing two figures, which a headless Octave cannot do.
  reference.m tolerates that one error and rethrows any other, then checks that
  every captured variable exists, so a run truncated before the numbers are
  computed fails instead of being captured.

Source
  Released replication package for
    Li, Kathleen T. (2024), "Forward Difference-in-Differences,"
    Marketing Science 43(2). DOI 10.1287/mksc.2022.0212
